<h2>2. What this site is and is not</h2>

<p>Proof &amp; Machine is an independent editorial publication about artificial intelligence and mathematics. It publishes essays, explanations and analysis.</p>

<p>It is not a source of professional advice of any kind. Nothing here is educational advice, engineering advice, investment advice, legal advice or a recommendation to adopt or avoid any product, technology or approach. Articles discuss the capabilities and limitations of software systems, sometimes critically, and those discussions are opinion informed by evidence rather than professional guidance you should act on without your own judgement.</p>

<p>If you are making a decision with real consequences, in a classroom, on an engineering project, or anywhere money or safety is involved, consult someone qualified and accountable for that decision.</p>

<h2>3. Accuracy</h2>

<p>Articles are researched from primary sources and checked before publication. That process reduces errors and does not eliminate them.</p>

<p>Two categories of inaccuracy are inevitable and worth naming specifically.</p>

<p>Claims about the current capability of AI systems become stale quickly. A statement about how a model performs on a task is a statement about a particular version of a particular system at a particular time. Every article carries a publication date and you should read capability claims as scoped to that date.</p>

<p>Benchmark figures, record values and performance numbers are quoted from their sources and were accurate when checked. Records get broken, benchmarks get revised, and papers get corrected. Where we learn of a change, the article gets updated.</p>

<p>The site is provided as-is. No warranty is given that any content is accurate, complete, current or fit for any purpose. Corrections are welcome and handled per the process on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>

<h2>4. The step solver</h2>

<p>The calculator at <a href="/tool/">/tool/</a> runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you enter is transmitted anywhere, stored on any server, or seen by anyone.</p>

<p>It uses IEEE 754 double-precision floating point arithmetic, with all the limitations that implies. Decimal fractions are not represented exactly. Integers above 2 to the power of 53 lose precision. Repeated operations accumulate rounding error. The tool page documents these in detail and the factorisation function refuses inputs where the limits would produce silently wrong results.</p>

<p>The solver is provided as a demonstration and a convenience. Do not rely on it for anything consequential without independent verification, which is advice we would give about any calculator including the one on your phone.</p>

<p>The source is available at <code>/assets/solver.js</code> and may be reused freely, with or without attribution, including commercially. It is offered with no warranty of any kind. If it is wrong, it is wrong in your project too, and that is your risk to evaluate.</p>

